# Docker reads this file from the build CONTEXT ROOT, and its patterns are
# resolved relative to that root — not to this file's directory. In an
# lt-monorepo the context is the repo root, so `projects/app/.dockerignore` is
# never consulted at all. Every pattern that must also hit `projects/*` therefore
# uses a `**/` glob, so the same list works for a standalone build (context =
# this directory) and for a copy placed at the monorepo root. The Dockerfile
# additionally purges the build-read artefacts, so a missing root .dockerignore
# can never bake a developer's local config into an image.

# Dependencies
**/node_modules
**/.pnpm-store
**/.npm

# Build output. The globstar is load-bearing: docker anchors a slash-free
# pattern at the CONTEXT ROOT, so a bare `.nuxt` would exclude nothing once this
# template sits at `projects/app` in a monorepo.
**/.nuxt
**/.nuxt-check
**/.nuxt-test
**/.output
# DEV-2724: the `lt dev test` stack's own output tree, written only on developer
# machines — an axis of its own, independent of the build dirs above.
**/.output-test
# Catch-all beneath the named entries above. `NUXT_BUILD_DIR` / `NITRO_OUTPUT_DIR`
# make `.nuxt-<x>` / `.output-<x>` trivial to spawn — a manual `.nuxt-perf`
# profiling run is what prompted this, after `oxfmt` walked into `.output-perf` and
# the check chain died on a file it had no business formatting. The named entries
# stay: they carry the reason each specific dir exists, and
# tests/unit/nuxt-builddir-isolation.test.ts asserts them individually. This pair
# only catches what nobody wrote an entry for.
**/.nuxt-*
**/.output-*
**/dist

# IDE / tooling
**/.idea
**/.vscode
**/.claude
**/.husky
**/*.swp
**/*.swo

# Logs
**/*.log
# Rotated too: `**/*.log` only matches a segment ENDING in `.log`, so
# `.lt-dev/app.log.1` would sail straight past it.
**/*.log.*
**/npm-debug.log*

# `lt dev`'s runtime directory — detached server logs and scratch state. Written
# whenever the stack runs locally, never read by a build.
**/.lt-dev

# Testing. All three are gitignored, so they never appear in `git status` to
# remind anyone they are in the context — and a Playwright trace records the full
# request log of the failing test, session cookie included.
**/coverage
**/test-results
**/playwright-report
**/blob-report
**/.vitest
# The specs reach the BUILDER stage otherwise. Not a leak (the runner copies only
# `.output`), but it is context weight and one more thing that busts the
# `COPY ${APP_DIR}/` layer on every test edit.
**/tests
**/.turbo

# Environment — `nuxt build` reads .env, so a developer's local config would be
# baked into the image. `.env*` covers the whole family (`.env.production`, a
# hand-made `.env.bak`) instead of the three shapes that were listed here; a
# secret file is not worth enumerating shape by shape. `.env.example` goes with
# it, since no build step reads it either.
**/.env*

# Git
**/.git
**/.gitignore

# Docker
**/Dockerfile*
**/docker-compose*
**/.dockerignore

# Documentation
**/*.md
!README.md

# Misc
**/.DS_Store
**/*.tgz
